  "narrative": "<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Short impact summary</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Women in the Silent Cinema shows clear signs of active readership and open access reach. The book has been downloaded 1,654 times in the OAPEN metrics record, and it is available in gold open access, which supports broad reuse and discovery. The evidence for scholarly citation is still early: OpenAlex currently records 0 citations.</p>\n<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Full narrative</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Annette F\u00f6rster\u2019s Women in the Silent Cinema is a 2017 scholarly book from Amsterdam University Press that reconstructs the careers of women working across silent film and related stage worlds in the Netherlands, France, and North America. The record points to a work positioned for international academic and teaching use: it is openly available in gold open access, appears in repository networks, and has a WorldCat holding, indicating library discoverability.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The strongest impact signal is use rather than citation. OAPEN download metrics record 1,654 downloads, suggesting sustained consultation by readers. The monthly pattern includes a noticeable rise in one month of the record, followed by continued activity across the year, which is consistent with ongoing interest rather than a single isolated access event.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Scholarly citation impact remains limited in the available record, with OpenAlex showing no citations so far. That does not diminish the book\u2019s subject importance, but it does mean the evidence currently available is weighted more toward readership and access than toward citation-based influence. The book\u2019s open-access status and presence in multiple repository instances should help support future uptake.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Other signals are comparatively modest but still useful. The book has one WorldCat holding listed in the digest, which indicates at least some library discovery. The available data also show that dataset citation metadata were successfully processed, but the digest does not provide a clear count of resulting scholarly references. Public-discourse evidence is limited in this record, and event archive data are unavailable, so the most defensible narrative is one of visible access, early scholarly reception, and strong potential for continued circulation.</p>",
